          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>In organ transplantation, what is the primary risk when immunosuppressant drug l</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In organ transplantation, what is the primary risk when immunosuppressant drug levels fall BELOW the therapeutic range?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Post-transplant lymphoproliferative disorder</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Post-transplant lymphoproliferative disorder (PTLD) results from excessive immunosuppression, not insufficient suppression.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Acute allograft rejection</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>When drug levels are subtherapeutic, the recipient's immune system remains active enough to recognise donor tissue as foreign and mount a destructive T-cell and antibody response.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Opportunistic infections</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Opportunistic infections are a consequence of over-suppression of the immune system, not under-suppression — they occur when drug levels are too high.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Drug-induced nephrotoxicity</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Nephrotoxicity is an adverse effect of supratherapeutic drug levels (particularly with calcineurin inhibitors), not of subtherapeutic levels.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "When immunosuppressant levels fall below the therapeutic range, the recipient's immune system is insufficiently suppressed — it recognises donor tissue as foreign and mounts a destructive response, causing rejection."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! The therapeutic window is narrow — when levels are too low, the immune system attacks the graft. When too high, the risks shift to nephrotoxicity, opportunistic infections, and PTLD."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
