          "label": "<p>A junior doctor repeatedly uses 3% hydrogen peroxide to clean a chronic wound wi</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A junior doctor repeatedly uses 3% hydrogen peroxide to clean a chronic wound with healthy granulation tissue. What is the most likely adverse consequence of this practice?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Severe systemic hydrogen peroxide toxicity</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Systemic toxicity from topical 3% H₂O₂ is extremely rare — tissue catalase rapidly decomposes it locally into water and oxygen.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Promotion of anaerobic bacterial growth in the wound</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The release of oxygen at the wound surface works against anaerobes, not in their favour — H₂O₂ generates reactive oxygen species with antimicrobial activity.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Damage to granulation tissue, delaying wound healing</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>While useful for initial debridement, repeated 3% H₂O₂ application damages delicate granulation tissue and delays healing — it is an acute wound-cleaning agent, not for chronic wound management.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Induction of antimicrobial resistance in wound flora</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>H₂O₂ acts through non-specific oxidative damage — resistance development to reactive oxygen species is not a clinically significant concern at this concentration.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "While 3% H₂O₂ effectively cleans wounds by mechanically dislodging debris (via catalase-released O₂ bubbles), repeated application damages granulation tissue. It is an acute cleaning agent, not for chronic wound management where tissue regeneration must be preserved."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! Hydrogen peroxide at 3% is suited for initial wound debridement — catalase-generated oxygen bubbles dislodge debris — but repeated use damages granulation tissue and delays healing in chronic wounds."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
